// japanese-korean-demo — Japanese and Korean typesetting in texish.
//
// Render with:  texish-cli scripts/japanese-korean-demo.script
//
// Japanese and Korean join Chinese on the CJK path, each with its own bundled face,
// because Han (kanji / hanja) has region-specific letterforms and Korean adds the
// Hangul syllables the Chinese faces have no glyph for.
//
// Japanese sets like Chinese: it has no interword spaces, so the engine offers a break
// between adjacent characters and applies kinsoku (a closing mark never starts a line,
// an opening mark never ends one). Korean is different — it is written with spaces
// between words and its lines break at those spaces, the way Latin does, so its syllables
// are kept off the per-character break path and a word is never split across a line.
//
// \font japanese (alias \font cjkjp) and \font korean (alias \font cjkkr), each with a
// bold cut.

\use{document}

\title{Japanese and Korean in \TeXish}
\author{The texish engine}
\maketitle

\section{Japanese}

Japanese mixes kanji with the two kana syllabaries, hiragana and katakana. It is set with
its own face so the kanji take their Japanese forms rather than the Chinese ones. Like
Chinese it has no interword spaces, so the engine inserts the break opportunities between
characters and stretches them to justify, keeping closing marks off the start of a line and
opening marks off the end.

\medskip

{\font japanese 12 regular
これは日本語の組版の例です。テキシュ・エンジンは、空白のない一続きの文章を複数の行に分割し、
それぞれの行を右側の版面の端までそろえます。文字と文字のあいだには幅がゼロで伸縮する空きが
入るので、行末が版面からはみ出すことも、みっともない余白が残ることもありません。句読点の
禁則も守られます。句点や読点のような閉じ括弧「このような始め括弧も、行のはじめや終わりには
置かれず、となりの文字といっしょに動きます。}

\section{Korean}

Korean is written in Hangul, whose syllable blocks the Chinese faces do not carry at all.
It uses spaces between words, so — unlike Chinese and Japanese — its lines break at those
spaces, exactly as Latin does, and a word is never broken across a line. Hanja (Han
characters) appear occasionally and the same face sets them.

\medskip

{\font korean 12 regular
이것은 한국어 조판의 예입니다. 텍시 엔진은 한글로 쓰인 문단을 여러 줄로 나누되, 낱말의
사이에 있는 공백에서만 줄을 바꾸므로 한 낱말이 두 줄에 걸쳐 잘리는 일이 없습니다. 이는
라틴 문자를 다루는 방식과 같습니다. 한자를 섞어 쓸 수도 있는데, 예를 들어 大韓民國과 같은
낱말이 그러합니다.}

\medskip

\noindent The same paragraph wraps at its word spaces inside a narrower measure too:

\begin{quote}
{\font korean 12 regular
한글은 자음과 모음을 모아 하나의 음절 글자를 이루며, 낱말과 낱말 사이는 공백으로 띄어
씁니다. 그래서 줄바꿈은 그 공백에서 일어납니다.}
\end{quote}

\section{A closing line}

A greeting in each, set in the bold cut:

\medskip

{\font japanese 16 bold こんにちは！}\quad{\font korean 16 bold 안녕하세요!}
